haemoglobin, and by nothing else.” Had he discovered a gold mine, greater delight could not have shone upon his features.\n\n> “Dr. Watson, Mr. Sherlock Holmes,” said Stamford, introducing us.\n\n> “How are you?” he said cordially, gripping my hand with a strength for which I should hardly have given him credit. “You have been in Afghanistan, I perceive.”\n\n> “How on earth did you know that?” I asked in astonishment.\n\n> “Never mind,” said he, chuckling to himself. “The question now is about haemoglobin. No doubt you see the significance of this discovery of mine?”\n\n> “It is interesting, chemically, no doubt,” I answered, “but practically⁠—”\n\n> “Why, man, it is the most practical medico-legal discovery for years. Don’t you see that it gives us an infallible test for blood stains? Come over here now!” He seized me by the coat-sleeve in his eagerness, and drew me over to the table at which he had been working. “Let us have some fresh blood,” he said, digging a long bodkin into his finger, and drawing off the resulting drop of blood in a chemical pipette. “Now, I add this small quantity of blood to a litre of water. You perceive that the resulting mixture has the appearance of pure water. The proportion of blood cannot be more than one in a million. I have no doubt, however, that we shall be able to obtain the characteristic reaction.” As he spoke, he threw into the vessel a few white crystals, and then added some drops of a transparent fluid. In an instant the contents assumed a dull mahogany colour, and a brownish dust was precipitated to the bottom of the glass jar.\n\n> “Ha! ha!” he cried, clapping his hands, and looking as delighted as a child with a new toy. “What do you think of that?”\n\n> “It seems to be a very delicate test,” I remarked.\n\n> “Beautiful! beautiful! The old Guiacum test was very clumsy and uncertain. So is the microscopic examination for blood corpuscles. The latter is valueless if the stains are a few hours old. Now, this appears to act as well whether the blood is old or new. Had this test been invented, there are hundreds of men now walking the earth who would long ago have paid the penalty of their crimes.”\n\n> “Indeed!” I murmured.\n\n> “Criminal cases are continually hinging upon that one point. A man is suspected of a crime months perhaps after it has been committed. His linen or clothes are examined, and brownish stains discovered upon them. Are they blood stains, or mud stains, or rust stains, or fruit stains, or what are they? That is a question which has puzzled many an expert, and why? Because there was no reliable test. Now we have the Sherlock Holmes’ test, and there will no longer be any difficulty.”\n\nHis eyes fairly glittered as he spoke, and he put his hand over his heart and bowed as if to some applauding crowd conjured up by his imagination.\n\n> “You are to be congratulated,” I remarked, considerably surprised at his enthusiasm.\n\n> “There was the case of Von Bischoff at Frankfort last year. He would certainly have been hung had this test been in existence. Then there was Mason of Bradford, and the notorious Muller, and Lefevre of Montpellier, and Samson of New Orleans. I could name a score of cases in which it would have been decisive.”\n\n> “You seem to be a walking calendar of crime,” said Stamford with a laugh. “You might start a paper on those lines. Call it the *Police News of the Past*.”\n\n> “Very interesting reading it might be made, too,” remarked Sherlock Holmes, sticking a small piece of plaster over the prick on his finger. “I have to be careful,” he continued, turning to me with a smile, “for I dabble with poisons a good deal.” He held out his hand as he spoke, and I noticed that it was all mottled over with similar pieces of plaster, and discoloured with strong acids.\n\n> “We came here on business,” said Stamford, sitting down on a high three-legged stool, and pushing another one in my direction with his foot. “My friend here wants to take diggings, and as you were complaining that you could get no one to go halves with you, I thought that I had better bring you together.”\n\nSherlock Holmes seemed delighted at the idea of sharing his rooms with me. “I have my eye on a suite in Baker Street,” he said, “which would suit us down to the ground. You don’t mind the smell of strong tobacco, I hope?”\n\n> “I always smoke ‘ship’s’ myself,” I answered.\n\n> “That’s good enough. I generally have chemicals about, and occasionally do experiments. Would that annoy you?”\n\n> “By no means.”\n\n> “Let me see⁠—what are my other shortcomings? I get in the dumps at times, and don’t open my mouth for days on end. You must not think I am sulky when I do that. Just let me alone, and I’ll soon be right. What have you to confess now? It’s just as well for two fellows to know the worst of one another before they begin to live together.”\n\nI laughed at this cross-examination. “I keep a bull pup,” I said, “and I object to rows because my nerves are shaken, and I get up at all sorts of ungodly hours, and I am extremely lazy. I have another set of vices when I’m well, but those are the principal ones at present.”\n\n> “Do you include violin-playing in your category of rows?” he asked, anxiously.\n\n> “It depends on the player,” I answered. “A well-played violin is a treat for the gods⁠—a badly-played one⁠—”\n\n> “Oh, that’s all right,” he cried, with a merry laugh. “I think we may consider the thing as settled⁠—that is, if the rooms are agreeable to you.”\n\n> “When shall we see them?”\n\n> “Call for me here at noon tomorrow, and we’ll go together and settle everything,” he answered.\n\n> “All right⁠—noon exactly,” said I, shaking his hand.\n\nWe left him working among his chemicals, and we walked together towards my hotel.\n\n> “By the way,” I asked suddenly, stopping and turning upon Stamford, “how the deuce did he know that I had come from Afghanistan?”\n\nMy companion smiled an enigmatical smile. “That’s just his little peculiarity,” he said. “A good many people have wanted to know how he finds things out.”\n\n> “Oh! a mystery is it?” I cried, rubbing my hands. “This is very piquant. I am much obliged to you for bringing us together. ‘The proper study of mankind is man,’ you know.”\n\n> “You must study him, then,” Stamford said, as he bade me goodbye. “You’ll find him a knotty problem, though. I’ll wager he learns more about you than you about him. Goodbye.”\n\n“Goodbye,” I answered, and strolled on to my hotel, considerably interested in my new acquaintance.","draft_number":1,"id":"29ad560b-076e-46d8-a42e-5dc728fafcd0","name":null,"title":"Chapter 1: Mr Sherlock Home"}],"notebooks":[{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Brigham Young confronts Ferrier: by Church law Lucy must marry within the faith, either Drebber's son or Stangerson's son, within a month. Ferrier, who has secretly vowed Lucy will never wed a Mormon, refuses.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":169,"icon":null,"id":"8ddc85e8-666b-47af-8583-7ea25c704365","row_index":3,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 3: John Ferrier Talks with the Prophet"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Ferrier smuggles word to Hope, who returns and guides father and daughter on a desperate night escape across the mountains.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":140,"icon":null,"id":"4bae14b4-9235-41e1-ae01-0ea3494b5622","row_index":4,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 4: A Flight for Life"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"While Hope is away hunting food, the Danites overtake the fugitives. They kill Ferrier and drag Lucy back; she is forced to marry Drebber and dies of a broken heart within a month. Hope swears revenge and stalks the two men for years, until they flee Utah and the chase scatters across Europe and back to London.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":200,"icon":null,"id":"116438ce-c496-4ade-934c-875d8efc2287","row_index":5,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 5: The Avenging Angels"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Hope dies of his aneurysm before trial. Holmes walks Watson through the full chain of reasoning. Gregson and Lestrade take the public credit; Watson resolves to set the true record down himself.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":155,"icon":null,"id":"71bd1419-0c7a-4c4e-a338-98853225ac69","row_index":7,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 7: The Conclusion"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Back in custody, Hope tells his story: a cab driver in London, he tracked Drebber and Stangerson, gave Drebber a lethal choice between two pills (one poison, one harmless), and stabbed Stangerson when he fought back. He has an aortic aneurysm and knows he is dying.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":178,"icon":null,"id":"519155ad-ad6d-4012-ae29-2ded7871842b","row_index":6,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 6: A Continuation of the Reminiscences of John Watson, M.D."},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Army surgeon invalided home from Afghanistan; the book is framed as his memoir. Steady, loyal, a touch unworldly, and the reader's stand-in, bowled over by Holmes's deductions. Key life events:\n\n1. 1878: Takes M.D. at the University of London; trains at Netley.\n2. 1878–80: Posted to India, then the Second Afghan War with the Berkshires.\n3. 27 Jul 1880: Wounded at the Battle of Maiwand (Jezail bullet, shoulder).\n4. Late 1880: Struck by enteric fever; invalided home on the troopship *Orontes*; lands at Portsmouth on an army pension.\n5. Winter 1880–81: Adrift in a London hotel; meets Stamford; is introduced to Holmes.\n6. 1881: Moves into 221B Baker Street; witnesses the Lauriston Gardens case.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":396,"icon":null,"id":"cb7cde7a-3f45-4ce7-af72-fb7a899fb364","row_index":0,"theme":"sage-green","title":"🩺 Dr. John H. Watson — narrator"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Scotland Yard. Holmes calls him one of the smartest of the Yarders, which is faint praise. Energetic, ambitious, vain; jumps to a confident wrong arrest.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":152,"icon":null,"id":"db53b33b-56f7-4dce-9cf6-667353237114","row_index":1,"theme":"clean-white","title":"👮 Inspector Tobias Gregson"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"American silver prospector turned hunter, trapper, and finally a London cab driver. Lucy's lover; after her death he devotes his life to killing the two men responsible. Resourceful, relentless, physically powerful, dying of an aneurysm. Not a villain so much as a wronged man. The novel withholds sympathy for him until Part II reframes everything.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":203,"icon":null,"id":"37b8b214-11d4-4b70-8569-9140d08b54b6","row_index":1,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🚬 Jefferson Hope — antagonist / tragic avenger"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Holmes advertises the found ring as bait. The caller is a wizened old woman who claims it for her daughter and slips away. Holmes tails her, only to find the killer used a decoy in disguise.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":165,"icon":null,"id":"20e1999c-5e8e-446c-82f0-2db2181965dc","row_index":5,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 5: Our Advertisement Brings a Visitor"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Holmes, having quietly identified the killer, lures him to Baker Street and claps the cuffs on a cab driver Jefferson Hope. The capture closes the London mystery; the motive is still unknown.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":152,"icon":null,"id":"449f111b-5a09-46d9-80af-660e5e00283d","row_index":7,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 7: Light in the Darkness"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Gregson proudly arrests the wrong man (Arthur Charpentier) on a false lead. News breaks that Drebber's secretary, Joseph Stangerson, has been found stabbed at a hotel, **RACHE** again above him, with two pills beside the body.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":174,"icon":null,"id":"f2c76781-1326-461b-bfc8-e9cf57425d07","row_index":6,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 6: Tobias Gregson Shows What He Can Do"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Consulting detective; the world's first, by his own account. Tall, lean, hawk-nosed; alternates between feverish energy and torpid stillness. Knowledge is deep but deliberately narrow (chemistry, sensational crime, anatomy) and famously blank on anything he deems useless. Cold, vain, theatrical, but fair. Plays violin; uses the Baker Street street urchins as agents.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":237,"icon":null,"id":"ac55a02c-bb21-4504-8284-19788591f34f","row_index":0,"theme":"mocha","title":"🕵️ Sherlock Holmes"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Scotland Yard. Lean, ferret-like, dogged. Rival to Gregson; equally outpaced by Holmes. ","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":144,"icon":null,"id":"469caa2f-0fe3-4c6b-8825-09354b2e76a1","row_index":2,"theme":"clean-white","title":"👮 Inspector Lestrade"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"The beat policeman who found Drebber's body and, without realizing it, met the murderer at the scene.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":145,"icon":null,"id":"ea18fb0a-5bf1-40ac-8fed-069a043acbe8","row_index":3,"theme":"clean-white","title":"👴 Constable John Rance"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"American, of Cleveland, Ohio. As a young man in Utah, one of the suitors forced on Lucy; he \"won\" her and effectively caused her death. A coarse, dissolute drunk in his later years. Hope's first kill.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":159,"icon":null,"id":"87181291-219e-4261-acee-45e8aa7f02cb","row_index":2,"theme":"clean-white","title":"💀 Enoch J. Drebber — victim"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"The man rescued on the Alkali Plain; adoptive father to Lucy. Becomes a wealthy, respected farmer but privately refuses to let Lucy marry within the Church. Murdered by the Danites for that defiance.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":169,"icon":null,"id":"eb730b5b-f8b2-407a-98af-c04627e633f4","row_index":4,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🧓 John Ferrier"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Orphan rescued as a child; grows into \"the Flower of Utah.\" Loves Jefferson Hope; forced to marry Drebber after her father's murder; dies within a month. The emotional center the whole revenge plot turns on.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":165,"icon":null,"id":"30860dba-457c-4d50-824c-66dacf18dde2","row_index":5,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🌸 Lucy Ferrier"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"The Prophet and leader of the Mormon settlement (a real historical figure used as a character). Rescues the Ferriers, then later delivers the ultimatum that destroys them. Drawn as benevolent founder and implacable theocrat.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":163,"icon":null,"id":"16e5bf70-15db-46de-882a-81252fb2abca","row_index":4,"theme":"clean-white","title":"📿 Brigham Young"},{"chapter_id":"34fa1ec7-f4ff-4a7d-b727-cc57bcdfb125","col_index":0,"content":"Watson's friend, the man who introduced him to Holmes.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"d36ea579-fc2f-4b37-a825-22a3a38a553f","height":200,"icon":"👨‍⚕️","id":"912e9ce2-662b-46c9-94d7-b00b0edd7d15","row_index":0,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Stamford"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Leader of Holmes's gang of street urchins, hired as cheap, invisible eyes across London.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":149,"icon":null,"id":"2f0d176d-d79e-471a-aed1-35bce844c840","row_index":6,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🧒 Wiggins & the Baker Street division"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"The official police. Competent at legwork, hopeless at inference; embodied by the bickering rivals Gregson and Lestrade. Foil to Holmes's method throughout.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"7edc7582-26ac-4c9c-8db0-3a24eeee4e15","height":305,"icon":null,"id":"ede9253e-b958-4240-9d5a-ee8db8760917","row_index":0,"theme":"midnight","title":"🚓 Scotland Yard"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":2,"content":"Holmes's network of London street children, led by Wiggins. Cheap, fast, and beneath notice. They go where the police can't.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"7edc7582-26ac-4c9c-8db0-3a24eeee4e15","height":304,"icon":null,"id":"e8c2b65d-b7d5-4450-a627-f350334e3bc2","row_index":0,"theme":"mocha","title":"🧒 The Baker Street Irregulars"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":3,"content":"\"Avenging Angels\" The Church's secret enforcers in the novel, a shadow order that silences dissenters. They murder Ferrier and recapture Lucy. The story's instrument of fear.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"7edc7582-26ac-4c9c-8db0-3a24eeee4e15","height":304,"icon":null,"id":"1d5c1785-a787-410e-9061-40c288b29515","row_index":0,"theme":"blush","title":"🗡️ The Danite Band"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"The frontier theocracy under Brigham Young. Rescuer turned oppressor: it saves the Ferriers, then claims absolute authority over Lucy's marriage.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"7edc7582-26ac-4c9c-8db0-3a24eeee4e15","height":303,"icon":null,"id":"05a7eb3d-e5d9-4142-8ce5-4540101038e2","row_index":0,"theme":"sepia","title":"📿 The Latter-day Saints"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"The empty house, south of the river, where Drebber's body is found with RACHE on the wall. *Map position: south London.*","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"8fd13141-d974-4af6-b005-4cd46c082150","height":153,"icon":null,"id":"e415a397-6ab1-4fc0-b688-413c63a928ed","row_index":0,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🩸 3 Lauriston Gardens, off the Brixton Road"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Holmes and Watson's shared lodgings in Marylebone; the base of operations. *Map position: north-west central London.*","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"8fd13141-d974-4af6-b005-4cd46c082150","height":154,"icon":null,"id":"6e119fc5-99ef-4c5e-ac03-b898b3399297","row_index":0,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🏠 221B Baker Street"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"The hotel (near Euston, in Little George Street) where Stangerson is found stabbed. *Map position: north-central London.*","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"8fd13141-d974-4af6-b005-4cd46c082150","height":148,"icon":null,"id":"26e8962f-8fed-4cdc-8a6f-659dc5604120","row_index":1,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🏨 Halliday's Private Hotel"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"The chemical laboratory where Stamford first introduces Watson to Holmes. *Map position: City of London, east-central.*","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"8fd13141-d974-4af6-b005-4cd46c082150","height":155,"icon":null,"id":"32de65e0-1657-4410-8945-dc42ca7d61af","row_index":2,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🔬 St Bartholomew's Hospital (Bart's)"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Drebber's secretary and the other forced suitor from Utah; complicit in Ferrier's murder. Hope's second kill, stabbed when he resisted the pills.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":158,"icon":null,"id":"1a0091f6-5e02-481c-8ed5-0c54a8b36ad5","row_index":3,"theme":"clean-white","title":"💀 Joseph Stangerson — victim"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Madame Charpentier's boarding house, Drebber's last London address. *Map position: south London.*","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"8fd13141-d974-4af6-b005-4cd46c082150","height":150,"icon":null,"id":"924bfdec-dc96-4c2d-ba35-cbe003a9874e","row_index":3,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🏡 Torquay Terrace, Camberwell"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"4 May 1847. On the Sierra Blanco, a starving man, John Ferrier, and a small orphaned girl, Lucy, are the last survivors of a lost wagon party. A Mormon caravan led by Brigham Young rescues them on the condition they adopt the faith.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":168,"icon":null,"id":"3ede2d1f-0b47-4310-b675-ce8aad6e66b9","row_index":1,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 1: On the Great Alkali Plain"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Constable Rance's lodgings, where Holmes interviews him. *Map position: south London.*","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"8fd13141-d974-4af6-b005-4cd46c082150","height":140,"icon":null,"id":"d25f51e4-0be3-4335-b793-52ba0c3098f0","row_index":3,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🏚️ Audley Court, Kennington"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Home turf of Gregson and Lestrade. *Map position: Whitehall, Westminster, central.*","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"8fd13141-d974-4af6-b005-4cd46c082150","height":131,"icon":null,"id":"8b6ef0b5-329e-4ec2-a62d-9cc103010211","row_index":2,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🚓 Scotland Yard"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Piccadilly; where Watson runs into Stamford. *Map position: central west.*","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"8fd13141-d974-4af6-b005-4cd46c082150","height":129,"icon":null,"id":"2efbbc68-4a99-4cca-8665-c85b9378fc6a","row_index":1,"theme":"clean-white","title":"🍸 The Criterion Bar"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Keeper of the Camberwell boarding house where Drebber lodged, and her son was wrongly arrested by Gregson. A red herring.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"5c1ed792-6d13-4d86-bc04-1636fc9cefa6","height":155,"icon":null,"id":"6792a237-6f78-4a39-8684-066017b75f4a","row_index":5,"theme":"clean-white","title":"👩 Madame Charpentier & Arthur Charpentier"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Years on, Salt Lake City thrives and Ferrier is a prosperous farmer. The grown Lucy meets a young silver prospector, Jefferson Hope, and they fall in love. He leaves on a job, intending to return and marry her.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":162,"icon":null,"id":"24a50ef0-5e01-4be7-9435-5936c0202ae2","row_index":2,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 2: The Flower of Utah"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"Part II : The Country of the Saints","font":"serif","font_size":"xl","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":154,"icon":null,"id":"187619d0-6825-49e4-a0db-9e858e42e6dc","row_index":0,"theme":"forest","title":""},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":1,"content":"**4 May 1847: Rescue on the Alkali Plain** John Ferrier and the child Lucy, last survivors of a lost wagon train, are found near death on the Sierra Blanco by Brigham Young's westbound caravan and taken in as Mormons.\n\n**1847 (mid–late): Founding of the settlement** The caravan reaches the valley and establishes Salt Lake City.\n\n**1847–1860: Ferrier prospers** Ferrier becomes a wealthy farmer; Lucy grows up. He privately vows she will never marry within the Church.\n\n**1860: Lucy and Jefferson Hope fall in love** Hope, a passing silver prospector, courts Lucy and leaves on a job, meaning to return and marry her.\n\n**1860: The Prophet's ultimatum** Brigham Young orders Lucy to marry Drebber's or Stangerson's son within one month.\n\n**1860: The flight and Ferrier's murder** Hope returns and leads the family's night escape; while he hunts for food, the Danites overtake them, kill Ferrier, and recapture Lucy.\n\n**1860: Lucy's forced marriage and death** Lucy is married to Drebber and dies within a month. Hope swears vengeance.\n\n**1860–1881: The twenty-year pursuit** Hope hunts Drebber and Stangerson across America and Europe as they flee, eventually reaching London.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"44a12ac9-bad0-4ad9-8505-09be92a71857","height":702,"icon":null,"id":"08c334d7-39a6-485e-8ff3-a873531c3690","row_index":0,"theme":"soft-blue","title":"Part II"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"**January 1880: Watson meets Holmes** Introduced by Stamford at Bart's; the two agree to share 221B Baker Street.\n\n**4 March 1881: Watson reads \"The Book of Life\"** Watson reads the anonymous article and learns Holmes wrote it; Holmes explains consulting detection.\n\n**5 March 1881: The Lauriston Gardens murder** Drebber is found dead in the empty house; RACHE on the wall; Holmes reads the scene.\n\n**6 March 1881: The decoy and the second murder** The ring advertisement is foiled by a disguised accomplice; Stangerson is found stabbed at Halliday's Hotel; Gregson arrests the wrong man.\n\n**7 March 1881: Capture of Jefferson Hope** Holmes lures the cab-driver killer to Baker Street and arrests him.\n\n**10 March 1881:** Hope's aortic aneurysm kills him in custody the morning he is due before the magistrates. Holmes lays out the full solution","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"44a12ac9-bad0-4ad9-8505-09be92a71857","height":702,"icon":null,"id":"cbfa66bb-e4b8-4cff-8a05-700ee041def5","row_index":0,"theme":"navy","title":"Part I"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Watson catalogues his baffling flatmate, encyclopaedic on some subjects, blank on others. He reads an anonymous magazine article, \"The Book of Life,\" arguing a whole life can be read from trifles, and is annoyed to learn Holmes wrote it. Holmes reveals he is a \"consulting detective.\" A telegram arrives.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":200,"icon":null,"id":"f3b68bac-c0af-413b-a5b3-dd75d6a0c99a","row_index":2,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 2: The Science of Deduction"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Invalided home from the Afghan war, Watson is broke and adrift in London. An old acquaintance, Stamford, introduces him to an eccentric researcher looking to share rooms. Watson and Holmes agree to take 221B Baker Street together.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":182,"icon":null,"id":"4169a622-a6c7-444b-9795-768b8b22d658","row_index":1,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 1: Mr. Sherlock Holmes"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Holmes interviews the constable who found the body and extracts the detail the police missed: a \"drunk\" man loitering at the scene, almost certainly the murderer, returned for the ring.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":168,"icon":null,"id":"0a66a5ec-13e3-4332-a63c-79e362d2a88a","row_index":4,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 4: What John Rance Had to Tell"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Inspector Gregson asks Holmes's help with a body in an empty house off the Brixton Road. The victim is Enoch Drebber of Cleveland, unmarked, no robbery, a woman's wedding ring nearby, and **RACHE** scrawled on the wall in blood. Holmes reads the scene and predicts the killer's height, build, and habits.","font":"inter","font_size":"medium","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":200,"icon":null,"id":"d8db5328-90f0-4b9e-b2ff-b95da8cb1177","row_index":3,"theme":"clean-white","title":"Chapter 3: The Lauriston Garden Mystery"},{"chapter_id":null,"col_index":0,"content":"Part I: Being a Reprint from Reminiscences of John H. Watson, M.D.","font":"serif","font_size":"xl","grid_id":"3fcc7117-7321-4575-b05a-f3c4a5b22862","height":154,"icon":null,"id":"5ab6dca4-462d-4022-9951-fcabc4f86917","row_index":0,"theme":"slate","title":""}],"maps":[{"bg_color":"#f2e8e0","cells":{"-1,162":"#d9c0aa","-1,163":"#d9c0aa","-1,62":"#d9c0aa","-1,63":"#d9c0aa","-1,91":"#d9c0aa","-1,92":"#d9c0aa","-10,137":"#d9c0aa","-10,138":"#d9c0aa","-10,62":"#d9c0aa","-10,63":"#d9c0aa","-100,10":"#d9c0aa","-100,137":"#d9c0aa","-100,138":"#d9c0aa","-100,9":"#d9c0aa","-101,10":"#d9c0aa","-101,137":"#d9c0aa","-101,138":"#d9c0aa","-101,9":"#d9c0aa","-102,10":"#d9c0aa","-102,137":"#d9c0aa","-102,138":"#d9c0aa","-102,9":"#d9c0aa","-103,10":"#d9c0aa","-103,137":"#d9c0aa","-103,138":"#d9c0aa","-103,9":"#d9c0aa","-104,10":"#d9c0aa","-104,137":"#d9c0aa","-104,138":"#d9c0aa","-104,9":"#d9c0aa","-105,10":"#d9c0aa","-105,137":"#d9c0aa","-105,138":"#d9c0aa","-105,9":"#d9c0aa","-106,10":"#d9c0aa","-106,137":"#d9c0aa","-106,138":"#d9c0aa","-106,9":"#d9c0aa","-107,10":"#d9c0aa","-107,137":"#d9c0aa","-107,138":"#d9c0aa","-107,9":"#d9c0aa","-108,10":"#d9c0aa","-108,137":"#d9c0aa","-108,138":"#d9c0aa","-108,9":"#d9c0aa","-109,10":"#d9c0aa","-109,137":"#d9c0aa","-109,138":"#d9c0aa","-109,9":"#d9c0aa","-11,137":"#d9c0aa","-11,138":"#d9c0aa","-11,62":"#d9c0aa","-11,63":"#d9c0aa","-110,10":"#d9c0aa","-110,137":"#d9c0aa","-110,138":"#d9c0aa","-110,9":"#d9c0aa","-111,10":"#d9c0aa","-111,137":"#d9c0aa","-111,138":"#d9c0aa","-111,9":"#d9c0aa","-112,10":"#d9c0aa","-112,137":"#d9c0aa","-112,138":"#d9c0aa","-112,9":"#d9c0aa","-113,10":"#d9c0aa","-113,137":"#d9c0aa","-113,138":"#d9c0aa","-113,9":"#d9c0aa","-114,10":"#d9c0aa","-114,137":"#d9c0aa","-114,138":"#d9c0aa","-114,9":"#